    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d180">

                <pb facs="0397" n="351" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg351"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/985½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-20">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">20 January, 1918</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: The suggestion made by
                    the Japanese government in this despatch<anchor n="33" xml:id="d180-f1"/> seems
                    to me very significant of possible coming events, and I would be very much
                    obliged to you if you would tell me what reply you think should be made to
                    it.</p>
                <p>The fact that the Japanese are sending a larger naval force to Vladivostok than
                    they at first led us to expect makes an uncomfortable impression on me,
                    particularly in view of this latest request.</p>
                <p>It seems to me clear that we should show very clearly in our reply that we should
                    look upon military action in that quarter with distinct disapproval.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
